--TEST--
Core\Str::compare orders every ordered pair of a table the way strcmp, strcasecmp, strnatcmp and strnatcasecmp order it, in all four combinations of its two options, and it answers -1/0/1 in every cell where strcasecmp still answers a byte difference (spec § 1 Comparison)
--FILE--
<?mwl
// Spec § 1's *Comparison* row folds PHP's four comparison functions into one
// member with two independent options, so what is worth pinning is not four
// separate agreements but that the *fold* is exact: each of the four corners
// of `{caseInsensitive, natural}` is one of PHP's functions and nothing in
// between. A member that grew its own comparison for one corner still prints
// a plausible column on its own, and fails here.
//
// `compare` is also the only way two strings are ordered at all — `<` over
// two `string`s does not lower, so there is no operator column to check this
// against. That makes the exactness of the fold the whole contract.
//
// What is compared cell by cell is the *sign*, because PHP's four do not
// agree with each other on the magnitude: 8.2 narrowed `strcmp` to -1/0/1
// and `strnatcmp`/`strnatcasecmp` were always that, but `strcasecmp` on this
// leg's 8.5.9 still returns the byte difference — 84 of this table's 144
// case-insensitive cells are values like `-8`, `48` and `65`. A magnitude is
// not part of the contract in either language (nothing may do arithmetic on
// it), so normalizing is the comparison, and the last block below is where
// MWL's own narrowed return is asserted rather than assumed.
//
// Every word is ASCII on purpose. `{caseInsensitive: true}` folds through
// Unicode's simple lower-case mapping (`mwl_stdlib::str`'s `folded`) where
// `strcasecmp` is byte-wise, and `{natural: true}` skips Unicode whitespace
// where `strnatcmp` skips ASCII's; inside ASCII the two agree, which is what
// this file is entitled to compare, and outside it MWL is deliberately the
// better of the two.
final class Show {
    // A cell is inside PHP 8.2's narrowed range if it is one of exactly three
    // values. `Core\Math::abs` would say the same thing in one call; three
    // named values say it without a second member in the claim.
    public static function narrowed(int $n): bool {
        if ($n == -1) {
            return true;
        }
        if ($n == 0) {
            return true;
        }
        return $n == 1;
    }
}

// The empty string, a letter in both cases, a prefix and its extension, the
// natural-sort classics where a numeric run has to beat a lexical one, a
// leading zero, a leading space, and a bare numeral. Every ordered pair of
// these is a cell, so the diagonal pins reflexivity and the two triangles
// pin antisymmetry without either being written as its own row.
array<string> $words = ["", "a", "A", "ab", "abc", "img2", "img12", "IMG10", "a01", "a1", " 9", "10"];

// `{}` — `strcmp`. Byte order, which is where `A` sorts before `a`.
foreach ($words as string $left) {
    foreach ($words as string $right) {
        echo Core\Str::compare($left, $right), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

// `{caseInsensitive: true}` — `strcasecmp`. The pairs that move are exactly
// the ones that differ only in case; `IMG10` against `img12` is the cell
// that shows the fold happening before the comparison rather than after.
foreach ($words as string $foldLeft) {
    foreach ($words as string $foldRight) {
        echo Core\Str::compare($foldLeft, $foldRight, {caseInsensitive: true}), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

// `{natural: true}` — `strnatcmp`, quirks included, because the spec names
// that function as what the option replaces and a program being migrated is
// entitled to the order it already sorts in. `a01` against `a1`, `img2`
// against `img12` and `" 9"` against `10` are the three quirks: a leading
// zero is skipped, a digit run compares as a number, and whitespace is
// skipped on both sides before every character.
foreach ($words as string $natLeft) {
    foreach ($words as string $natRight) {
        echo Core\Str::compare($natLeft, $natRight, {natural: true}), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

// Both options — `strnatcasecmp`. The corner that would be easiest to reach
// by folding after the natural comparison rather than during it.
foreach ($words as string $bothLeft) {
    foreach ($words as string $bothRight) {
        echo Core\Str::compare($bothLeft, $bothRight, {caseInsensitive: true, natural: true}), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

// Antisymmetry, counted over every cell of all four tables rather than read
// off a row: reversing the arguments negates the answer. A comparison that
// resolved a tie by argument position — which is what `mwl_stdlib::math`'s
// `pick` does, and what parts `Core\Math::min` from PHP's — would still
// print four plausible tables and fail this line. PHP's four hold it too.
int $mirrored = 0;
int $pairs = 0;
foreach ($words as string $symA) {
    foreach ($words as string $symB) {
        $pairs = $pairs + 4;
        if (Core\Str::compare($symA, $symB) + Core\Str::compare($symB, $symA) == 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::compare($symA, $symB, {caseInsensitive: true}) + Core\Str::compare($symB, $symA, {caseInsensitive: true}) == 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::compare($symA, $symB, {natural: true}) + Core\Str::compare($symB, $symA, {natural: true}) == 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::compare($symA, $symB, {caseInsensitive: true, natural: true}) + Core\Str::compare($symB, $symA, {caseInsensitive: true, natural: true}) == 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $mirrored, " of ", $pairs, "\n";

// The range, which is the one claim here that is about MWL alone: every cell
// of all four tables is one of exactly three values, by construction, where
// `strcasecmp` on this leg answers a byte difference in most of them. The
// PHP side of this block counts its own normalized values and so is
// trivially whole — it is here to make the count comparable, and the claim
// it carries is that `compare`'s `Ordering` never reaches the caller as
// anything but the three literals `mwl_stdlib::str` maps it to. Counted
// rather than eyeballed off the tables above, because a single cell
// answering `-3` is invisible in a hundred and forty-four of them.
int $inRange = 0;
int $counted = 0;
foreach ($words as string $rangeA) {
    foreach ($words as string $rangeB) {
        $counted = $counted + 4;
        if (Show::narrowed(Core\Str::compare($rangeA, $rangeB))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (Show::narrowed(Core\Str::compare($rangeA, $rangeB, {caseInsensitive: true}))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (Show::narrowed(Core\Str::compare($rangeA, $rangeB, {natural: true}))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (Show::narrowed(Core\Str::compare($rangeA, $rangeB, {caseInsensitive: true, natural: true}))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $inRange, " of ", $counted, "\n";
--ORACLE--
<?php
function sign($n) {
    if ($n < 0) {
        return -1;
    }
    if ($n > 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

function narrowed($n) {
    if ($n === -1) {
        return true;
    }
    if ($n === 0) {
        return true;
    }
    return $n === 1;
}

$words = ["", "a", "A", "ab", "abc", "img2", "img12", "IMG10", "a01", "a1", " 9", "10"];

foreach ($words as $left) {
    foreach ($words as $right) {
        echo sign(strcmp($left, $right)), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

foreach ($words as $foldLeft) {
    foreach ($words as $foldRight) {
        echo sign(strcasecmp($foldLeft, $foldRight)), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

foreach ($words as $natLeft) {
    foreach ($words as $natRight) {
        echo sign(strnatcmp($natLeft, $natRight)), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

foreach ($words as $bothLeft) {
    foreach ($words as $bothRight) {
        echo sign(strnatcasecmp($bothLeft, $bothRight)), ",";
    }
    echo "\n";
}

$mirrored = 0;
$pairs = 0;
foreach ($words as $symA) {
    foreach ($words as $symB) {
        $pairs = $pairs + 4;
        if (sign(strcmp($symA, $symB)) + sign(strcmp($symB, $symA)) === 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (sign(strcasecmp($symA, $symB)) + sign(strcasecmp($symB, $symA)) === 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (sign(strnatcmp($symA, $symB)) + sign(strnatcmp($symB, $symA)) === 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (sign(strnatcasecmp($symA, $symB)) + sign(strnatcasecmp($symB, $symA)) === 0) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $mirrored, " of ", $pairs, "\n";

$inRange = 0;
$counted = 0;
foreach ($words as $rangeA) {
    foreach ($words as $rangeB) {
        $counted = $counted + 4;
        if (narrowed(sign(strcmp($rangeA, $rangeB)))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (narrowed(sign(strcasecmp($rangeA, $rangeB)))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (narrowed(sign(strnatcmp($rangeA, $rangeB)))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
        if (narrowed(sign(strnatcasecmp($rangeA, $rangeB)))) {
            $inRange = $inRange + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $inRange, " of ", $counted, "\n";
